.TH SLIP0039 1 "November 2026" "slip0039" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slip0039 \- split a secret into SLIP-0039 shares and recover it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slip0039
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fBrecover\fR]
.br
.B slip0039
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fBcheck\fR
.br
.B slip0039
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fBsplit\fR \fIEXP\fR \fIGT\fR \fIXofY\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slip0039
implements SLIP-0039, Shamir's Secret-Sharing for Mnemonic Codes. It splits
a master secret, protected by a passphrase, into groups of shares of 20 or
more words, and recovers the master secret from enough of them.
.PP
All input is read from standard input, one item per line. If standard
input is a terminal, every item is asked for, and the passphrase, the seed
and the master secret are not echoed.
.PP
.B split
is deterministic: the shares are a function of the master secret, the
passphrase and the
.IR SEED .
.B verify.py
in the source reproduces them independently.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B recover
(the default) reads the passphrase and then the shares, one per line, and
prints the master secret. Empty lines are skipped, upper case, extra
spaces and CRLF line ends are accepted and a word can be given by its first
four letters. All shares are checked: a share of another split is
detected, also if it is not needed.
.TP
.B check
reads the shares, without a passphrase, and reports which groups are
complete and which shares are missing. If there are enough shares, it
checks that they belong together. The exit status is 0 if the master
secret can be recovered.
.TP
\fBsplit\fR \fIEXP\fR \fIGT\fR \fIXofY\fR...
reads the passphrase, the
.I SEED
and the master secret and prints the shares, group by group.
.I EXP
is the iteration exponent (at most 15), the encryption takes
2500*2^\fIEXP\fR iterations of PBKDF2 per round.
.I GT
is the number of groups that is needed, and every
.I XofY
describes a group: X of its Y shares are needed, as in 2of3.
The
.I SEED
should be a long random string (64 characters or more) that is used once;
it can be generated with
.BR \-\-random\-seed ,
or by leaving it empty on a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fICODEC\fR[\fB:\fR[\fIWORDLIST\fR][\fB:\fR\fIARG\fR]]
The format of the master secret. The argument
.I ARG
is not stored in the shares, write it down with them;
.B split
prints the command to recover.
.TP
\fB\-\-gain\fR \fIGAIN\fR
The bound on the advantage that an attacker gets from the honey encoding
of structured secrets, the default is 0.01.
.TP
.B \-\-random\-seed
With
.BR split :
generate the
.I SEED
(64 characters), it is not read from the input. It is printed on standard
error; it is needed only to check the shares with
.BR verify.py .
.TP
.B \-\-pre\-may\-2024
With
.BR split :
make shares in the format of SLIP-0039 before May 2024, without the
extendable backup flag. Not recommended.
.B recover
accepts both formats without this option.
.TP
.B \-d
Show the internal data (shares, digests, secrets) on standard error at
exit.
.TP
.B \-q
Do not show warnings and notes.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH CODECS
With most codecs every master secret, so also the one that a wrong
passphrase gives, decodes to a valid secret of the same kind (honey
encryption).
.TP
.B base16
16 to 64 bytes in hexadecimal, a multiple of 2 bytes (the default).
.TP
\fBbip39\fR[\fB:\fR\fILANGUAGE\fR]
A BIP39 mnemonic of 12, 15, 18, 21 or 24 words. LANGUAGE is english (the
default), spanish, french, italian, portuguese, czech, japanese, korean,
chinese_simplified or chinese_traditional. The input is converted to
Unicode normalization form NFKD.
.TP
\fBdiceware:\fR[\fILANGUAGE\fR]\fB:\fR\fIWORDS\fR
A diceware passphrase of WORDS words, english (the default), dutch or
german.
.TP
\fBpassword:\fR[\fIALPHABET\fR]\fB:\fR\fILENGTH\fR
A random password, the alphabet is alnum (the default), alpha, lower or
ascii.
.TP
\fBnumeric::\fR\fIDIGITS\fR
A random number of DIGITS digits, a PIN for example.
.TP
\fBtotp::\fR\fICHARS\fR
The base32 secret of a TOTP or HOTP authenticator, CHARS characters
without padding.
.TP
\fBbip32::\fR\fIVERSION\fR
A BIP32 root extended private key, VERSION is xprv, yprv, zprv, Yprv,
Zprv, tprv, uprv, vprv, Uprv or Vprv.
.TP
\fBwif::\fR\fITYPE\fR
A bitcoin private key in Wallet Import Format, TYPE is compressed,
uncompressed, testnet-compressed or testnet-uncompressed.
.TP
.B nostr
A nostr private key (nsec1...).
.TP
\fBcodex32::\fR\fIHEADER\fR
A codex32 secret, HEADER is its threshold and identifier, as in 0test.
.SH SECURITY
The process locks its memory (it needs a few MiB of locked memory, see
.BR "ulimit \-l" ),
disables core dumps and wipes all secrets at exit, also on a fatal signal.
Honey encryption only helps against an attacker who has no other way to
check a guess; the keys of a wallet that has been used can be checked
against the blockchain.
.SH EXAMPLES
Split a master secret into 2 of 3 shares and recover it from two of them:
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'TREZOR\en%s\en%s\en' "$SEED" 00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff |
    slip0039 split 0 1 2of3 > shares
$ (echo TREZOR; sed -n 1,2p shares) | slip0039
00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff
.fi
.PP
Split a PIN, with a generated seed:
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'TREZOR\en042195\en' | slip0039 -c numeric::6 --random-seed split 0 1 2of3
.fi
.PP
Check shares without the passphrase:
.PP
.nf
$ slip0039 check < shares
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error or, with
.BR check ,
if there are not enough shares.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/satoshilabs/slips/blob/master/slip-0039.md,
the README and verify.py in the source of slip0039.
.SH AUTHOR
Rik Snel <rik@snel.it>
